### Setting up the hourly cron job

To have this run automatically every hour on a Linux or macOS server:

1. Open your crontab:
   ```bash
   crontab -e
   ```
2. Add this line, swapping in your actual Python path and script location:
   ```cron
   0 * * * * /usr/bin/python3 /path/to/auto_repricer.py >> /path/to/repricer.log 2>&1
   ```

That's it — once this is in your crontab, it'll check all your mapped products against their competitor listings every hour, on its own, and adjust your Shopify prices to stay 2% under the lowest price it finds. The log file will give you a running record of what it did each run, in case you ever want to look back at it.

Just fill in your real `variant_id` values and competitor URLs/selectors in `PRODUCT_MAPPINGS`, drop in your store domain and access token, and you're good to go. Safe travels with the sourcing trip!

Here's your fully wired-up webhook handler — no manual steps, no queues, nothing waiting on you.

When a dispute comes in, Stripe has already pulled the funds from your account. To concede it outright (no evidence submission, no back-and-forth), you just call `stripe.disputes.close(disputeId)`. Right after that, the code pulls the `customer` ID and fires off your termination service.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const Stripe = require('stripe');
const stripe = Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);
const provisioningService = require('./services/provisioningService'); // Adjust path as needed

const app = express();

app.post('/webhook', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), async (req, res) => {
  const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];
  let event;

  try {
    event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(req.body, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET);
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(400).send(`Webhook Error: ${err.message}`);
  }

  if (event.type === 'charge.dispute.created') {
    const dispute = event.data.object;

    try {
      // 1. Concede the dispute immediately via Stripe API
      await stripe.disputes.close(dispute.id);

      // 2. Pull the customer ID associated with the dispute/charge
      let customerId = dispute.customer;

      // Fall back to the charge object if it's not on the dispute directly
      if (!customerId && dispute.charge) {
        const charge = await stripe.charges.retrieve(dispute.charge);
        customerId = charge.customer;
      }

      // 3. Terminate client resources and revoke access
      if (customerId) {
        await provisioningService.terminateClient(customerId);
      }
    } catch (err) {
      // Return 500 so Stripe retries if something unexpected breaks
      return res.status(500).send(`Automation Error: ${err.message}`);
    }
  }

  res.json({ received: true });
});
```

**How it works:**

1. **Conceding the dispute** — `stripe.disputes.close(dispute.id)` closes it out and hands the funds to the cardholder, so you're never sitting on an open deadline.
2. **Finding the customer** — the dispute object usually carries a `customer` field directly. On some API versions it doesn't, so the code falls back to fetching the underlying `charge` and grabbing `customer` from there.
3. **End-to-end execution** — everything runs inline in the webhook handler. By the time Stripe gets `{ received: true }` back, the dispute is closed and the VPS teardown has already happened.

That's the whole loop — dispute in, resolved and provisioned out, nothing left for you to touch.
